# Question: does a container registry entry of the configuration, alone,
# decide how the image store reaches a registry: does a fetch of the short
# name hello succeed through the mirror the docker.io entry names, on a
# loopback address and port over plain HTTP, with no test key set
# (spec-container-4-operator-surface, table S-3: transport and mirror)?
#
# A unit test cannot decide it: the answer is what the config loader and its
# schema, the delivery of the container root to the plugin, the image store's
# part of the config transaction and the fetch worker do together, from the
# config file and the CLI the operator types.
#
# Method: the compiled registry fixture serves one small image on the
# loopback port $PORT2, under library/hello:latest, with the hub's anonymous
# bearer challenge. The daemon runs with no fixture store named, so the image
# store package answers, with its data directory under /run, never in the
# test's work directory: in the QEMU guest that directory is on the 9p share
# of the checkout. ze.test.container.registry.port is NOT set: without the
# registry entry the fetch would dial the public hub over TLS and fail. The
# config holds the entry for docker.io, the registry the short name hello
# completes to, with transport http and a mirror at 127.0.0.1 port $PORT2,
# and the SSH server ze cli reaches. The fetch must answer exit 0, and the
# listing must hold the hub's whole reference, the state present and a
# digest.
option=needs-linux:caps=sys-admin
option=exclusive:group=container
option=timeout:value=60s
option=env:var=ze.data.dir:value=/run/ze-container-registry-$PORT
option=env:var=ze.ssh.username:value=admin
option=env:var=ze.ssh.password:value=testpass
option=env:var=ze.ssh.port:value=$PORT
tmpfs=running.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
system {
	authentication {
		# bcrypt cost-4 hash of "testpass".
		user admin {
			password "$2a$04$UlwuiuH82Unfsq.XEMPGJeDkXwbm3KW.nvVaVXOd/JeFK8VjMjrQO"
		}
	}
}
environment {
	ssh {
		enabled true
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1;
			port $PORT;
		}
	}
}
container {
	registry docker.io {
		transport http
		mirror {
			address 127.0.0.1
			port $PORT2
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=le test fixture container/registry $PORT2:name=registry:timeout=58s:ready=REGISTRY: listening on
cmd=background:seq=2:exec=ze start running.conf:name=daemon:ready=Ze running.
cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=ze cli -c "create container image reference hello":timeout=45s:exit=0
cmd=foreground:seq=4:exec=ze cli -c "show container image list | json":exit=0
expect=stdout:contains=docker.io/library/hello:latest
expect=stdout:pattern="state":\s*"present"
expect=stdout:pattern="digest":\s*"sha256:[0-9a-f]{64}"
